  about: 'Somewhere around three thousand years ago, in the middle of the most sacred body of poetry humanity has produced — between hymns to fire and dawn and the unanswerable — a man sat down and described, with unbearable honesty, what it is to be an addict. He knows exactly what the dice have cost him: his wife, his family, his standing, his peace. He can see the whole wreckage clearly. And he cannot stop. The Gambler\'s Lament is the oldest first-person account of addiction on earth, and nothing written since has improved on its central, devastating insight — that the addict is not a person who fails to understand the damage, but a person who understands it perfectly and returns anyway. It is here in the Veda not as scripture to obey but as a human being to recognize. The hymn even resolves, in its final verses, into something like a recovery: put down the dice, plough your field, go home. Whether the speaker managed it, the poem does not say.',
