      commentary: 'The hymn opens not with regret but with the truth the addict cannot say in company: it feels good. He reaches immediately for the holiest comparison his world offers — Soma, the sacred draught the gods themselves drink — and lays it beside a handful of nuts rattling on the dirt. That is the first honest thing about addiction, and the hardest for outsiders to hear: the thing destroying him is genuinely, chemically joyful. The dice are jāgṛvi — "wakeful," sleepless — and so is he when they call. Notice the poet does not begin by blaming himself. He begins by naming the pull as a real force acting on him, the way a current acts on a swimmer. Everything else in the hymn — the ruin, the resolve, the relapse — follows from this refusal to pretend the hook isn\'t sweet.'
