      commentary: 'The hymn ends with the oldest recovery advice on record, and its wisdom is precisely that it is small. Not "renounce the world," not "conquer your soul" — just: plough your field, and count what you already have as enough. The Sanskrit bahu manyamānaḥ means "considering it much" — the cure for the gambler\'s endless craving is a deliberate act of re-valuing what is already in his hands. It is the same medicine the Isha Upanishad will later prescribe to everyone ("enjoy through letting go; whose is wealth, anyway?") — contentment as a discipline, not a feeling that arrives on its own. And notice the frame: the advice is placed in the mouth of Savitar, the god who impels and sets things in motion. The one power that hooked him was the dice; the counter-power the poet reaches for is a god of honest work and daily motion. The way out of compulsion, the hymn suggests, is not willpower against the pull but a different pull — a field, a home, a morning that asks something ordinary of you. Whether the gambler took it, the poem, with its final honesty, does not tell us.'
